Consider a dynamical system that is open to its environment and is permitted to modify its own governing rules during its evolution. Following the hierarchy of novelty introduced for open-ended systems, classify changes into three levels: (level 0) variation of the values of state variables under fixed rules; (level 1) introduction of new state variables, new entities, or new components ("innovations") within an existing model; and (level 2) transformations of the model itself, i.e., changes to the rules, the state space, or the very ontology in which the system is described. Define an open-ended self-modifying open system as one that continually produces level-1 and level-2 novelties, not merely level-0 variation.

Problem: Develop a mathematical formalism (together with associated computational tools) capable of modelling such a system. Specifically, exhibit a framework — e.g., based on rewriting systems, reflective/metacircular languages, category-theoretic constructions with morphisms acting on the category of models, hierarchical metamodels, or other structures — that
(i) represents the current state, rules, and ontology of the system as mathematical objects;
(ii) allows the system's own dynamics to alter not only the state but also the rules and the ontology, producing genuine level-1 innovations and level-2 transformations;
(iii) accommodates openness to an environment whose interactions can drive or trigger such self-modification;
(iv) supports formal analysis (e.g., of consistency, expressivity, or long-term behaviour) and admits an effective computational implementation.

Determine whether such a formalism exists and characterize its essential ingredients.

